    <item rdf:about="http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=aboriginal_pedagogy&amp;rev=1774002384&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2026-03-20T10:26:24+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>aboriginal_pedagogy</title>
        <link>http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=aboriginal_pedagogy&amp;rev=1774002384&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>8 Aboriginal Ways of Learning

Aboriginal Pedagogy

What is Pedagogy?

Pedagogy refers to the methods and practices of teaching. It’s about how we help others learn effectively.

Importance of Indigenous Pedagogy

Indigenous pedagogy is important because it incorporates traditional knowledge, culture, and respect for the environment. The goal is to make learning meaningful and connected to real life. Indigenous pedagogy focuses on community, relationships, and learning from the land, making educ…</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=aboriginal_seasons&amp;rev=1774002384&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2026-03-20T10:26:24+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>aboriginal_seasons</title>
        <link>http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=aboriginal_seasons&amp;rev=1774002384&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Understanding Seasonal Knowledge of First Nations Peoples

Introduction: Aboriginal Seasons

First Nations people have deep knowledge about the environment. All the seasons are mainly based on environmental changes rather than specific months. Every season marks important ecological events like plant flowering, animal behavior, and weather patterns.</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=about_us&amp;rev=1779750672&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2026-05-25T23:11:12+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>about_us</title>
        <link>http://16.176.2.91/doku.php?id=about_us&amp;rev=1779750672&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Welcome to this Wiki

This wiki is part of Indig[e]nizing ICT curriculum: a starter framework for the community of practice, a collaborative initiative aimed at making computing and digital technologies education more inclusive, relevant, and reflective of First Nations perspectives and knowledge systems.</description>
    </item>
